{
  "content": "### Updated cheatsheet\n\n### 1) Rebuild the active spec from user turns only, with the latest wording as the strongest update\n- Start from the **current user ask**, then reconcile it with earlier user turns only to the extent they still apply.\n- Treat the latest explicit user wording as the **authoritative contract update**: it can narrow, add to, or replace prior scope.\n- Maintain a running ledger of user-stated constraints, but **prune obsolete branches** when later wording makes them irrelevant.\n- Do not let earlier assistant framing keep extra requirements alive; if the user narrowed the task, analyze against the narrowed spec, not the old broader one.\n- Once the user\u2019s turns fully determine the task, stop searching for alternate task classes or speculative clarification paths.\n\n### 2) Separate mandatory requirements from unresolved choices\n- Mark user-stated conditions as mandatory when they specify required fields, entities, filters, scope, counts, ordering, or extremal conditions.\n- Distinguish **must be included** from **one possible way to satisfy the request**. If the user did not specify tie behavior, exact count, or one-vs-many handling, treat that as an unresolved gap rather than silently choosing.\n- Preserve constraints that still apply, but re-check them after every new user message.\n- When the user adds a new condition like \u201calso include X,\u201d treat it as accumulation unless the wording clearly replaces an earlier requirement.\n- If ambiguity remains after reconciling the user turns, name it as an **unresolved spec gap** rather than pretending it was settled.\n\n### 3) Treat output shape as a first-class part of the spec\n- Verify the requested **artifact shape** separately from semantic correctness: exact fields, granularity, identity level, ordering, aliases, counts, and packaging.\n- A response can be logically correct and still diverge by returning extra columns, summaries, multiple variants, grouped results, or reordering the user never asked for.\n- When the user narrows to \u201cjust\u201d a subset, a superset is usually scope creep even if it is schema-valid or join-correct.\n- Compare the final answer against the **current user ask**, not against earlier broader intent.\n- Separate **selection logic** from **returned projection**: the right rows with the wrong fields is still a mismatch.\n\n### 4) Watch for scope drift, invented subproblems, and anchoring on obsolete breadth\n- Diagnose when the assistant is solving an **invented subproblem** created by earlier elaboration instead of the latest user request.\n- Earlier complex framing can anchor later turns; verify whether the current user wording still needs that machinery. If not, compact it away.\n- If the user repeatedly asks for a simpler or narrower answer, treat extra breadth as drift rather than helpful completeness.\n- Prior correct work should be reused only if it still matches the current spec; otherwise it is an anchoring hazard.\n- When assistant-produced lists, examples, or optional variants are not explicitly selected by the user, treat them as noise.\n\n### 5) Prefer minimal, user-grounded analysis and flag only residual mismatches\n- Build a checklist of user constraints and mark each as satisfied, partially satisfied, or missing.\n- After each turn, compare the assistant\u2019s latest response against the **current cumulative spec** and flag only what remains off.\n- If the latest user message adds one new field or requirement, do not re-litigate everything else that was already correct.\n- Focus corrective attention on the specific clause, field, or formatting element that changed.\n- Prefer compacting the context to the **minimal sufficient active contract** before judging the next response.\n\n### 6) Recognize incremental disclosures as one finalized spec, not a timeline of alternatives\n- When the user adds another attribute, relation, or detail of the same entity, preserve the existing selection logic and verify that the output expands exactly as requested.\n- Accumulate user requirements into a single consolidated spec, then compare the assistant\u2019s response against that ledger.\n- If the assistant has the right core structure but misses a newly requested item, treat that as incomplete output, not a need to re-derive the whole plan.\n- Distinguish **entity pairing** from **entity enrichment**: if the user asked for one pairing, extra metadata is divergence unless explicitly requested.\n- Treat relation logic and output completeness as separate checks so a response can be judged partially correct without missing the newly added piece.\n\n### 7) Resolve ambiguity from user wording and examples when possible; otherwise name the gap\n- Use the user\u2019s own wording, examples, and cumulative constraints to disambiguate before inventing interpretations.\n- Referencing phrases like \u201cthese\u201d or \u201cfocus on\u201d should be grounded in the latest explicit user constraints, not earlier assistant assumptions.\n- When a choice is still genuinely unspecified, report it as unresolved rather than overcommitting to one interpretation.\n- Distinguish semantic alignment from presentation alignment: the right underlying result can still be projected or labeled incorrectly.\n\n### 8) Stay schema-grounded and reject unsupported assumptions\n- Map user terms to available schema or context, but do not invent missing fields, categories, thresholds, or behaviors to make the request feel complete.\n- Treat interpretations that depend on unsupported structure as speculative and therefore a potential divergence.\n- If earlier turns guessed a metric or filter and the user later clarified, the clarification replaces the guess.\n- Semantically equivalent forms are acceptable when the user did not constrain syntax; only flag syntax when style itself was required.\n\n### 9) Judge divergence by contract mismatch, not by whether the solution is \u201cgood\u201d\n- A response can be well-reasoned yet still fail because it returns too many fields, keeps an abandoned branch alive, or encodes an unsupported assumption.\n- Do not over-penalize stable parts that still match the user contract; focus on the changed or missing piece.\n- Extra joins, counts, variants, or explanation scaffolding are unnecessary baggage when the user asked for a narrower artifact.\n- The analyzer\u2019s job is constraint reconciliation: identify conflicts, omissions, and scope creep against the user-only spec, then point to the minimal correction needed.",
  "version": 4,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Sequence analysis around the latest user turn\n- Start by rebuilding the task spec from the **latest user message**, then merge earlier user constraints only if they still apply. Later user wording can fully replace earlier broad interpretations.\n- Treat the final user turn as a **constraint check**, not a prompt to invent a new solution path. Ask: \u201cDid this message add, narrow, or replace a requirement?\u201d\n- When a user adds a new constraint, classify it as **narrowing, widening, or superseding**. Remove superseded branches from the spec so older assumptions do not contaminate the analysis.\n- In multi-turn conversations, update the spec **turn by turn** rather than carrying forward the assistant\u2019s prior framing. Earlier assistant interpretations are provisional until confirmed by the user.\n\n### 2) Distinguish the current requirement from earlier partial alignment\n- Separate **\u201cdirectionally aligned\u201d** from **\u201cfully satisfies the current task.\u201d** A response can match the general theme yet still miss the latest constraint.\n- Track what is **already satisfied** versus what is **still pending**. If the latest user request is only a formatting, ordering, or narrowing change, do not flag unrelated parts that remain correct.\n- Detect when the current assistant output already satisfies the newest constraint; in that case, report no substantive issue rather than inventing one.\n- When the user progressively restricts the output, treat newer constraints as overriding broader ones and **remove no-longer-wanted fields, summaries, or dimensions** from the spec.\n\n### 3) Watch for output-shape drift and unnecessary structure\n- Check for **output-shape drift**: extra columns, metadata, counts, grouping, explanation, or other added structure after the user asked for a minimal projection.\n- Prefer the **minimal output shape** that still satisfies the latest user request. If the task only asks for a specific identity-level result, flag added aggregation or descriptive detail as potential drift.\n- Distinguish **necessary relational linkage** from **extra decoration**. Correct joins or associations can be aligned while added attributes remain out of scope.\n- Treat aggregation, concatenation, enumeration, or other compacting structure as suspect when the user\u2019s latest request is simpler and does not require it.\n- If the user asks about a subset of entities, verify the answer is about the **target entity type**, not its related records unless the user explicitly requested those records.\n\n### 4) Diagnose invented subproblems and anchoring hazards\n- Check whether the assistant is solving an **invented subproblem** introduced by earlier assistant framing rather than the user\u2019s latest constraint.\n- Treat earlier complex outputs as **anchoring hazards**: verify whether the final user request still needs counting, joining, grouping, windowing, or any other extra machinery.\n- Prefer the latest explicit user intent over prior assistant elaboration. If the user simplifies the task after a richer assistant proposal, the simplification controls.\n- Use previously correct work as reusable core only if it still matches the latest task; otherwise, **compact away** unneeded structure that could anchor future responses.\n\n### 5) Handle clarification and sufficiency carefully\n- Treat clarification-seeking as a risk when the user has already supplied enough information to determine the current requirement.\n- When the user\u2019s latest message does not add a new requirement, the correct analysis is usually **confirmation of alignment**, not a new solution plan.\n- Be conservative about raising issues that are merely alternative formulations; flag them only when they change correctness relative to the user\u2019s actual ask.\n\n### 6) Prefer exactness when the user is explicit\n- When the user provides explicit values, categories, thresholds, or scope boundaries, prefer **exact equivalence** over loose matching. Do not let a \u201croughly matches\u201d interpretation survive if the user has clarified the target.\n- Treat tentative interpretations as **provisional**. If the assistant inferred a threshold, range, or metric before the user clarified it, that inference should no longer be authoritative once the user speaks precisely.\n- Update the spec cumulatively when the user adds tighter requirements, and flag responses that omit the newest constraint as incomplete.\n\n### 7) Evaluate requested granularity and extremal requests directly\n- Distinguish **row-level context** from **requested output**. Context can be retained in the reasoning, but the final response must match the user\u2019s requested granularity.\n- If the user asks for an aggregate quantity, the output should be aggregate rather than row-level. If the user asks for specific names/entities, the output should collapse to that identity-level result rather than preserving unnecessary detail.\n- When the user asks for an extremum or \u201cmost/least\u201d style result, ensure the analysis checks whether the response computes that extremum **over the correct target measure** and at the correct scope.\n- Prioritize **omission detection** over stylistic complaints: missing a newly requested metric or extremum is more important than whether the query or response is elegantly structured.\n\n### 8) Use the user\u2019s own language to refine the spec\n- Infer the intended metric or category from the **user\u2019s wording and the available schema/context**, but do not preserve extra derived measures unless the user asked for them.\n- If the user changes from a broad summary to a specific instance-level or extremal request, treat that as a strong signal to discard broader summaries and focus on the exact requested output.\n- Keep the analysis grounded in the **minimal corrected intent** derived from user statements, not the assistant\u2019s intermediate reasoning path. This helps the next response start from the current specification, not from outdated assumptions.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the spec from user turns, with the latest turn as the strongest constraint\n- Build the task spec from **user messages only**, not from the assistant\u2019s prior framing. Treat earlier assistant interpretations as provisional.\n- Process turns **incrementally**: each new user message may add, tighten, or replace constraints. Merge them into a cumulative spec, but let the latest explicit wording override any broader earlier reading.\n- Classify new user statements as **additive, narrowing, or superseding**. Remove superseded branches so obsolete assumptions do not stay active.\n- Once the user\u2019s messages fully determine the intent, the analyzer should stop searching for alternate task classes or asking for extra clarification.\n\n### 2) Separate mandatory requirements from acceptable implementation choices\n- Track user-stated constraints as **hard requirements** when the wording makes them explicit: included fields, scope limits, filters, target entity type, granularity, or extremal conditions.\n- Distinguish **must be true** from **how it could be satisfied**. If the user has not specified tie behavior, exact output count, or one-vs-many handling, note the ambiguity rather than silently resolving it.\n- When the user adds a condition, verify it is reflected both in the final result and in any supporting logic or substructure the assistant uses to justify it.\n- Treat added conditions as cumulative unless the user explicitly narrows the scope. \u201cAlso include X\u201d should usually be read as adding X, not replacing prior requirements.\n\n### 3) Verify output shape separately from semantic correctness\n- Check whether the assistant returned the **requested artifact shape**: exact fields, exact granularity, exact quantity, or exact identity-level result.\n- Distinguish a correct relational path or filter from an incorrect output shape. A response can have the right selection logic but still miss required columns, include extra summaries, or return an overly broad result.\n- Watch for **output-shape drift**: extra columns, metadata, counts, alternative variants, explanatory bundles, grouping, or reordering that the user did not ask for.\n- Prefer the **minimal output** consistent with the user\u2019s current request. If the user narrowed the request to a single aggregate, identity, or final query, flag extra detail as scope creep.\n- Treat \u201cinclude/need X in the results\u201d as a direct output requirement, not merely a related fact that can appear elsewhere.\n\n### 4) Diagnose scope drift, invented subproblems, and anchoring hazards\n- Check whether the assistant is solving an **invented subproblem** introduced by earlier assistant elaboration instead of the user\u2019s latest request.\n- Earlier complex framing can anchor later turns; verify whether the final user wording still needs that machinery. If not, compact it away.\n- When the user simplifies a previously richer discussion, the simplification controls. Do not preserve abandoned alternatives, extra filters, or generalized families of answers.\n- Use prior correct work only if it still matches the current spec; otherwise treat it as an anchoring hazard rather than reusable progress.\n\n### 5) Distinguish ambiguity from unnecessary clarification\n- If the user\u2019s latest messages already determine the task class, extra clarification questions are usually divergence, not prudence.\n- If the conversation does not settle a remaining choice such as tie behavior, exact granularity, or whether multiple valid outputs are allowed, mark it as an **unresolved spec gap** instead of pretending it is solved.\n- Prefer resolving ambiguity from the user\u2019s own wording and the accumulated context before resorting to clarification-oriented interpretations.\n\n### 6) Use exact user wording to refine the spec, but stay schema-grounded\n- Map the user\u2019s terms to the available context or schema, but do not invent unsupported assumptions to make the request more specific than it is.\n- When the user gives a concrete qualifier, threshold, or scope boundary, prefer **exact equivalence** over approximate matches.\n- If earlier turns guessed a metric, category, or threshold and the user later clarified it, the clarification replaces the guess.\n- Distinguish semantic alignment from presentation alignment: the right data may still be formatted, aliased, or projected incorrectly.\n\n### 7) For multi-turn analysis, track what is already satisfied versus newly missing\n- Maintain a checklist of user constraints and mark each as satisfied, partially satisfied, or missing.\n- After each user turn, compare the assistant\u2019s latest response against the **current cumulative spec** and flag only the mismatches that remain.\n- If the latest turn only adds a small constraint, do not re-litigate everything else that was already correct.\n- When the assistant already has the right core structure, focus on residual mismatches rather than rewriting the whole approach.\n\n### 8) Prefer concise, user-grounded issue statements\n- Report what is wrong in terms of the user\u2019s wording and the current spec, not in terms of an implementation recipe.\n- Separate \u201caligned intermediate narrowing\u201d from \u201cfinal response correctness\u201d: a response can be directionally right yet still fail the latest constraint.\n- When compacting for an edit/rewrite setting, keep only the validated user constraints and the correct relational path; drop abandoned branches and assistant-side detours so the next step starts from the clean spec.",
    "### Updated cheatsheet\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the spec from user turns only, and let the latest user wording dominate\n- Build the task spec from **user messages only**; do not inherit assistant-side framing as if it were confirmed intent.\n- Update the spec **incrementally**: each user turn can add, narrow, or replace constraints. Re-evaluate the whole accumulated spec after every user message.\n- Treat the latest explicit user wording as the strongest constraint. If it narrows scope, simplify the spec accordingly and prune obsolete branches.\n- Classify new user statements as **additive, narrowing, or superseding**. Keep earlier constraints only if they still fit the latest ask.\n- Once the user messages fully determine the task, do not keep searching for alternate task classes or extra clarification paths.\n\n### 2) Separate hard requirements from optional implementation choices\n- Mark user-stated constraints as **mandatory** when they specify included fields, scope, target entity, filters, quantity, ordering, or extremal conditions.\n- Distinguish **must be true** from **one possible way to satisfy it**. If the user did not specify tie behavior, exact count, or one-vs-many handling, treat that as an unresolved spec gap rather than silently choosing.\n- Preserve validated constraints across turns, but re-check whether they are still active after each new user message.\n- If the user adds a condition like \u201calso include X,\u201d treat it as accumulation unless the wording clearly replaces an earlier requirement.\n\n### 3) Treat output shape as part of the spec, not a side effect\n- Verify the assistant\u2019s **requested artifact shape** separately from semantic correctness: exact fields, exact granularity, exact identity-level result, ordering, aliases, and counts.\n- A response can have the right filtering logic but still diverge by returning extra columns, summaries, grouping, metadata, or reordering that the user did not ask for.\n- When the user narrows the request to a subset of fields or to a simpler final artifact, extra detail is usually scope creep unless explicitly re-requested.\n- Compare the final output against the **current user ask**, not just the earlier, broader intent.\n- Treat \u201cinclude/need X in the results\u201d as a direct output requirement, not merely supporting context.\n\n### 4) Diagnose scope drift and invented subproblems\n- Check whether the assistant is solving an **invented subproblem** created by earlier assistant elaboration instead of the latest user request.\n- Earlier complex framing can anchor later turns; verify whether the latest user wording still needs that machinery. If not, compact it away.\n- If the user repeatedly asks for a simpler or more focused answer, treat extra breadth as drift rather than helpful completeness.\n- Use prior correct work only if it still matches the current spec; otherwise treat it as anchoring hazard rather than reusable progress.\n- When prior assistant questions created unneeded branches, compact toward the solvable core instead of preserving dead ends.\n\n### 5) Prefer minimal, user-grounded analysis and flag only residual mismatches\n- Maintain a checklist of user constraints and mark each as satisfied, partially satisfied, or missing.\n- After each turn, compare the assistant\u2019s latest response against the **current cumulative spec** and flag only the mismatches that remain.\n- If the latest user message adds only one new constraint, do not re-litigate everything else that was already correct.\n- When the assistant has the right core structure but misses a new narrowing condition, focus on that residual mismatch rather than rewriting the whole approach.\n- Prefer the **minimal corrected response** over the closest previous response; this reduces anchoring on obsolete additions.\n\n### 6) Resolve ambiguity from user language when possible; otherwise name the gap\n- If the user\u2019s latest messages already determine the task class, extra clarification is usually divergence, not prudence.\n- If a remaining choice is still genuinely unspecified, report it as an **unresolved spec gap** rather than pretending it has been solved.\n- Prefer resolving ambiguity from the user\u2019s own wording, examples, and cumulative constraints before resorting to speculative interpretations.\n- When the user uses referential phrases like \u201cthese\u201d or \u201cfocus on,\u201d resolve them from the latest explicit user constraints, not from earlier assistant assumptions.\n\n### 7) Stay schema-grounded and reject unsupported assumptions\n- Map the user\u2019s terms to the available schema or context, but do not invent missing fields, categories, or thresholds to make the request feel more specific.\n- Treat any interpretation that depends on unsupported structure as speculative and therefore a potential divergence.\n- If earlier turns guessed a metric, category, or filter and the user later clarified it, the clarification replaces the guess.\n- Distinguish semantic alignment from presentation alignment: the right underlying selection can still be projected, formatted, or aliased incorrectly.\n\n### 8) Recognize when a valid solution is still a spec mismatch\n- A response can be logically sound and still fail because it returns too many fields, encodes an unsupported assumption, or preserves an abandoned branch.\n- Do not over-penalize stable parts that still match the spec; focus on the clause, field, or constraint that actually changed.\n- Treat extra joins, counts, variants, or explanatory scaffolding as unnecessary baggage when the user asked for a narrower artifact.\n- The analyzer\u2019s job is constraint reconciliation, not solution generation: compare the assistant\u2019s approach against the user-only spec and identify conflicts, omissions, or scope creep."
